    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d521">

                <pb facs="0601" n="489" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg489"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/4342: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Vladivostok</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caldwell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vladivostok</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-04-21T13:00:00+08:47"><hi rendition="#italic">April 21, 1919,
                                1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 22, 7:26
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>239. Supplementing my cipher telegram number 230, April 17th [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">18th</hi>], 5 p.m. British cruiser <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Kent</hi> has returned from America Bay
                    where she fired about 60 shells, 6-inch, at persons presumed to be prepared to
                    resist landing of Omsk Government troops going to <pb facs="0602" n="490"
                        xml:id="pg490"/> rescue of comrades Vladimir Alexandrofka. Hostility against
                    Allies caused by such action by Great Britain will be felt not by Great Britain
                    but by others as she cannot be attacked on sea and apparently will not have
                    troops guarding railway.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
